{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_puzuedfieljusclrozwpycoixltrplo4drh6iwaocz7pik36zieq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## What was translated\n\nThe C library is the Wazuh alert-file monitor: 3 sources + 3 headers (696 lines total). All of it is translated, file-for-file:\n\n| C file | Rust file |\n|---|---|\n| `include/shared.h` | `src/shared.rs` |\n| `include/read-alert.h` + `src/read-alert.c` | `src/read_alert.rs` |\n| `include/file-queue.h` + `src/file-queue.c` | `src/file_queue.rs` |\n| `src/driver.c` | `src/driver.rs` |\n| — | `src/cbind.rs` (libc bindings) |\n\n## Completion gate: symbol diff is empty\n\n`nm -D` on the C `.so` vs the Rust `.so` — **identical, 9/9 symbols** (`diff` reported no differences on both name *and* type columns):\n\n```\nT FreeAlertData   T GetAlertData   T Init_FileQueue   T Read_FileMon   T driver\nT merror          T os_calloc      T os_realloc       T os_strdup\n```\n\nNo namespace/renaming macros exist in the headers; `merror` and the three `os_*` helpers are exported because `shared.h` *defines* them with external linkage in a header.\n\n## Byte-identical output — verified differentially\n\nI built a `dlopen`-based harness and ran it against both `.so` files, diffing stdout *and* stderr. **All byte-identical** (~2,300 lines of output, exit code 0 in both):\n\n- 12 hand-crafted alert logs + **400 fuzz iterations** over random alert fragments × random flag masks\n- All error paths: missing/unterminated comment quote, malformed `Rule:` line, both `perror` sites, missing `alerts.log`, `FSEEK_ERROR` on a FIFO (`errno 29`), `CRALERT_FP_SET` with caller-supplied `FILE*`, all 12 months, `timeout` loop (2 × 5 s `select` sleeps in both)\n- **`errno` evolution matches exactly** — the same `perror` call printed `: Success` vs `: Invalid argument` in the same places in both libraries\n\n## Faithfulness notes (bugs preserved, not fixed)\n\n- `merror` calls real `snprintf` with the caller's template, so 256-byte truncation and arbitrary format specifiers behave identically.\n- `atoi` reproduced as `strtol(...) as i32`, matching glibc's inline definition — verified: `\"4294967296\" → rule=0`, `\"99999999999\" → srcport=1215752191`, `\"-3\" → level=4294967293`.\n- `filename[strlen(filename) - 1] = '\\0'` **one-byte heap underflow** on an empty name is reproduced via `wrapping_add`, yielding `filename=[]` exactly as C does.\n- `strncpy(mon, ..., 3)` leaves `mon[3]` untouched (verified: `mon=[Jan?]` in both).\n- `log_size` never increments (upstream code is commented out), so the log branch is always taken — kept as-is.\n- Struct layouts verified against C `offsetof`/`sizeof`: `file_queue` 440 B (`fp`@288, `f_status`@296), `alert_data` 96 B, `struct stat` 144 B (`st_mtim`@88), `struct tm` 56 B.\n\nOne deliberate deviation: `s_month[p->tm_mon]` with an out-of-range month reads past a 12-element table (undefined behavior in C — I confirmed via `objdump` that `s_month` sits at the very end of `.data.rel.ro`, so the C result is unreproducible garbage or a crash). The Rust skips the copy instead; this is unobservable for valid months 0–11."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":857,"cache_read_input_tokens":98700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":857,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6f86ba7-a6f4-4dfd-afd7-2d600bfb0fb8","uuid":"e3033fb1-ad0a-4cf9-9c09-ffe30e618aca","timestamp":"2026-08-27T07:41:06.045Z"}
